<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TValueTriggeredCallback</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TValueTriggeredCallback" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TValueTriggeredCallback</tt> observes the value with <tt>PropertyName</tt> of a
control with <tt>ControlID</tt>. Changes to the observed
property value will trigger a new callback request. The property value is checked
for changes every <tt>Interval</tt> seconds.
A <tt>DecayRate</tt> can be set to increase the polling
interval linearly if no changes are observed. Once a change is
observed, the polling interval is reset to the original value.
</p>


<h2>Properties</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>ControlID</tt></td><td>The id of the control or element to watch.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>PropertyName</tt></td><td>The client-side property to read from it, such as <tt>value</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>Interval</tt></td><td>How often the value is read, in seconds. Defaults to 1.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>DecayRate</tt></td><td>How much the interval grows after each check that finds no change. Defaults to 0, meaning a fixed interval. A change resets the interval to its original value.</td></tr>
</table>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TTextBox ID="Search" /&gt;
&lt;com:TValueTriggeredCallback
    ControlID="Search"
    PropertyName="value"
    Interval="0.5"
    DecayRate="0.2"
    OnCallback="searchChanged" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
This watches a value rather than an event, so it notices a change however it was made,
including one written by other client script. The decay rate keeps an idle page from
polling as hard as an active one.
</p>

